I’ll first inspect the specimen metadata, pattern range/resolution, and the frozen analysis utilities/reference index. Then I’ll identify the ordinary chemistry-supported phases, build a replayable GSAS-II refinement, and only pursue residual families if the complete model still requires them.

The chemistry points first to CuWO₄, with unreacted CuO/WO₃ as the most plausible secondary phases. I’m now checking the supplied analysis interface and extracting the observed peak families before choosing any reference structures.

Three coherent families are now evident: triclinic CuWO₄, monoclinic WO₃, and cubic Cu₃WO₆. In particular, the Cu₃WO₆ family independently explains the 15.18°, 19.80°, 21.76°, 32.51°, and 33.76° reflections after the common specimen shift; this is broader evidence than a single coincident peak. I’m moving these three into a full-profile GSAS-II model, then I’ll check whether any CuO family remains.

The first full-profile branch is refining now. The initial GSAS-II setup exposed and I removed an otherwise redundant histogram-scale parameter, so the phase scales now carry the quantitative fractions without a scale degeneracy.

The three-phase model already reaches Rwp ≈ 8.17% before atomic or phase-specific profile refinement, with all three phase scales strongly positive. I’m now comparing the room-temperature monoclinic WO₃ references against the lower-symmetry checkpoint; this is important because a marginal numerical advantage for an over-flexible or thermally inappropriate WO₃ model would not be enough to select it.

CuO is a genuine fourth phase, not just an opportunistic extra component: it refines to a positive scale, a normal tenorite cell, and lowers Rwp from 7.95% to 7.20% by explaining the residual 35.1°/38.3°/61.2° family. The high-pressure CuWO₄ alternative fails broadly (Rwp ≈ 44%), while the ambient triclinic model is decisive. All coherent reflection families are now assigned, so I’m stopping phase/library search and refining this four-phase checkpoint.

The calibration/coordinate refinement is proceeding from the accepted four-phase checkpoint with profile terms frozen. Coordinates are being introduced in symmetry-constrained blocks (heavy atoms first, then oxygen) and each block is saved separately so any chemically bad geometry can be rolled back without losing the complete model.

All refined coordinate blocks passed the geometry screen: shortest W–O is 1.68 Å, Cu–O 1.91 Å, and O–O 2.35 Å or longer; the CuWO₄ and Cu₃WO₆ coordinate shifts also stay within about 0.05 Å. The profile stage is now separating phase-specific size/strain from the common instrumental width under positive physical bounds.

The supported profile/Uiso model now reaches Rwp 2.286% (GOF 1.42) with no frozen or boundary parameters: CuWO₄ size ≈ 0.18 μm, WO₃ microstrain ≈ 1664 ppm, and positive phase-wide Uiso values. I’m testing only bounded March–Dollase corrections now; any axis that does not improve a broad set of intensities with an interior ratio will be discarded.

The complete four-phase model is now at Rwp 2.29% with physical structures. I’m finishing the last bounded line-shape/specimen checks, then I’ll revalidate geometry and export the replayed final project and deliverables.

The preferred-orientation branches all returned essentially unity ratios and no material fit gain, so I’m excluding texture. I’m now checking the one remaining plausible specimen correction—low-angle surface roughness—against the complete model, using only bounded physical values.

Surface roughness was rejected: all starts ran to bounds or became highly correlated without improving the fit. A modest but stable WO₃ uniaxial microstrain along [001] did improve Rwp to 2.26%, with positive interior values; I’m retaining that supported term and now letting each accepted structure make one final bounded coordinate adjustment.

The first coordinate pass lowered Rwp from 2.26% to 1.86%, and the resulting bond geometry is sound (shortest W–O 1.73–1.80 Å, Cu–O 1.92–1.95 Å, O–O ≥2.44 Å). Some coordinate cycles had not fully converged, so I’m doing one final local pass from this physical checkpoint with the correct coordinate-shift bounds before freezing the structures.

The bounded convergence pass is complete: Rwp is 1.817%, all variables converged without freezing, and an independent GSAS-II forward replay reports no negative background or peak-width issues. I’m now exporting the exact replayed model and generating the normalized fractions/profile files from that same project.

Identified four crystalline phases, normalized over modeled crystalline material:

| Phase | Weight fraction |
|---|---:|
| CuWO₄ | 64.892 wt% |
| Cu₃WO₆ | 13.328 wt% |
| WO₃ | 19.148 wt% |
